  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 315 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nAdditional chances for rain Friday, details are uncertain:\n\nMuch like the discussion for tonight, there is uncertainty in\nthe location, timing, coverage, and hazards of showers and\nstorms Friday. Since each CAM is its own outlier for storm\nevolution tonight, each have a different solution Friday. Back\nto the basics of synoptic meteorology, though, the stationary\nfront continues to hang around, and with instability being no\nproblem, would expect at least isolated shower and thunderstorm\ndevelopment somewhere over our area (30-50% chance).\n\n\nHeat persists through next week:\n\nThe longwave trough axis finally moves east of our region\nSaturday and Sunday, making way for rising heights and\nincreasing temperatures. Highs over the weekend and into next\nweek are expected to be in the 90s, some areas approaching 100 \nF. Current dewpoint forecasts keep heat a tad lower than \n\"excessive\" with LREF ensembles showing <10% chance for >105 F \nHeat Index values. However, they do show >60% chances for >100 F\nvalues Saturday through at least Tuesday. So, while around 100 \nF Heat Index is not terribly uncommon for Ozarkian summers, the \nexpected extended nature of 100-105 F Heat Index values \nintroduces a Heat Risk as there will not be much room for heat \nrelief.\n\nWhile heat will build, the jet stream across the northern CONUS\nwill remain active. Some global models have the enhanced winds\ndropping south near our northern CWA. This may bring additional\nchances for rain and storms, but uncertainty in these types of\npatterns is too high for any appreciable mention above 15% until\nWednesday. As an example, the EFI does show values of around \n0.8 for CAPE-Shear combo in central and northern Missouri, \nwhich may highlight some potential for storms to come careening \ndown the northwest flow of the jet stream sometime next week,\nbut these are always mesoscale matters. Trends will continue to\nbe monitored.",
